# The same name thrown inside its own handler does not re-enter it: it goes to the enclosing
# context (§14).  m(0) throws Oops(0) into the inner handler, which throws Oops(1) through m(1); the
# enclosing catch takes it: r 101, n 1, 1011.  (Re-entering the inner handler instead would call
# m(2) and m(3), which returns 3: 33.)
fn: m (int: a) int
    throws: Oops
    if: a < 3
        throw: Oops(a)
return: a

int: r 0
int: n 0
---
    r: m(0)
    catch: Oops (int: x)
        n: n + 1
        r: m(n)
---
catch: Oops (int: o)
    r: 100 + o
return: r * 10 + n
